---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CategoryHub from '../../components/CategoryHub.astro';
import AffiliateDisclosure from '../../components/AffiliateDisclosure.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Event & auction software for nonprofits"
  description="Givebutter vs OneCause and the real cost of gala and auction software — when free event tools are enough and when dedicated auction software earns its fee."
  section="events-auctions">
  <div class="wrap">
    <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Events & auctions' }]} />
    <section class="prose" style="margin-top: 1.2rem;">
      <p class="kicker">Category guide</p>
      <h1>Event &amp; auction software</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Event software is the most seasonal line in the stack — you need it
        intensely for a few weeks a year. That's why the honest default is a free
        platform (Givebutter covers ticketing, auctions, and peer-to-peer at no
        platform cost with donor tips), with dedicated auction software
        (OneCause, from roughly $2,995/yr plus fees) reserved for shops whose
        gala is a six-figure production.
      </p>

      <h2>How to choose</h2>
      <p>
        Size the tool to the event, not the organization. A $20k community
        gala with a silent auction runs fine on Givebutter — mobile bidding,
        ticketing, and payments included, at zero platform fee. A $250k signature
        event with live auction, sponsorships, seating charts, and volunteer
        check-in crews is where OneCause-class software earns its package price:
        the fee is insurance against a chaotic night on your biggest revenue day.
      </p>
      <p>
        The math to run: dedicated auction platforms typically cost a base
        package plus ~5% of funds raised plus card processing. Against a $50k
        event, that's real money — against a $500k event, it's rounding. Decide
        at your event's size, not the vendor's demo.
      </p>

      <h2>The one mistake to avoid</h2>
      <p>
        Don't let the event tool become a shadow database. Export attendees,
        bidders, and donors into your CRM the week after the event — the
        follow-up gifts live there, not in last year's event platform.
      </p>
    </section>

    <CategoryHub category="events-auctions" />

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Events software stays free until <a href="/stacks/5m-plus/">the $5M+
        stack</a>, where a dedicated platform starts to justify itself.
      </p>
      <AffiliateDisclosure />
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
